{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-106756" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $A?$\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/state.json" "file_sha256" "0cb6e4900c271401c47fe4bb90f001e1935a8eb6bd27e69f260e4847c8b72e5d"} {"question" "q-106756" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $A,$ we write $A=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nA=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the left is not a unit lower triangular matrix, as its main diagonal contains a number not equal to $1.$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The second product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The third product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/state.json" "file_sha256" "0cb6e4900c271401c47fe4bb90f001e1935a8eb6bd27e69f260e4847c8b72e5d"} {"question" "q-106756" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3e0e5c10da6ead45c0e8bd01f0506e038c195bfddc01dae4e7fb2063d729afef"} {"question" "q-106756" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/state.json" "file_sha256" "0cb6e4900c271401c47fe4bb90f001e1935a8eb6bd27e69f260e4847c8b72e5d"} {"question" "q-106756" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/state.json" "file_sha256" "0cb6e4900c271401c47fe4bb90f001e1935a8eb6bd27e69f260e4847c8b72e5d"} {"question" "q-106756" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/state.json" "file_sha256" "0cb6e4900c271401c47fe4bb90f001e1935a8eb6bd27e69f260e4847c8b72e5d"} {"question" "q-75116" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $C?$\n\n- $C=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\n- $C=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\n- $C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/state.json" "file_sha256" "0cb6e4900c271401c47fe4bb90f001e1935a8eb6bd27e69f260e4847c8b72e5d"} {"question" "q-75116" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $C,$ we write $C=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nC=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is *not* in $LU$ form  because  the matrix on the left is not a unit lower triangular matrix, as its main diagonal contains a number not equal to $1.$\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The second product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although we have a product of an upper triangular matrix on the left and a unit lower triangular matrix on the right, the order of multiplication is not correct (instead, we need a unit lower triangular matrix on the left and an upper triangular matrix on the right).\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The third product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/state.json" "file_sha256" "0cb6e4900c271401c47fe4bb90f001e1935a8eb6bd27e69f260e4847c8b72e5d"} {"question" "q-75116" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3e0e5c10da6ead45c0e8bd01f0506e038c195bfddc01dae4e7fb2063d729afef"} {"question" "q-75116" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/state.json" "file_sha256" "0cb6e4900c271401c47fe4bb90f001e1935a8eb6bd27e69f260e4847c8b72e5d"} {"question" "q-75116" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II  and III only"] ["text" "II  only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/state.json" "file_sha256" "0cb6e4900c271401c47fe4bb90f001e1935a8eb6bd27e69f260e4847c8b72e5d"} {"question" "q-75116" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/state.json" "file_sha256" "0cb6e4900c271401c47fe4bb90f001e1935a8eb6bd27e69f260e4847c8b72e5d"} {"question" "q-75123" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $C?$\n\n- $C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$\n- $C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$\n- $C=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/state.json" "file_sha256" "0cb6e4900c271401c47fe4bb90f001e1935a8eb6bd27e69f260e4847c8b72e5d"} {"question" "q-75123" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $C,$ we write $C=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nC=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The second product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the left is not a unit lower triangular matrix, as it has a non-zero entry above the main diagonal. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The third product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the left is not a unit lower triangular matrix, as it has an entry not equal to $1$ on the main diagonal.\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/state.json" "file_sha256" "0cb6e4900c271401c47fe4bb90f001e1935a8eb6bd27e69f260e4847c8b72e5d"} {"question" "q-75123" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3e0e5c10da6ead45c0e8bd01f0506e038c195bfddc01dae4e7fb2063d729afef"} {"question" "q-75123" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/state.json" "file_sha256" "0cb6e4900c271401c47fe4bb90f001e1935a8eb6bd27e69f260e4847c8b72e5d"} {"question" "q-75123" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/state.json" "file_sha256" "0cb6e4900c271401c47fe4bb90f001e1935a8eb6bd27e69f260e4847c8b72e5d"} {"question" "q-75123" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/state.json" "file_sha256" "0cb6e4900c271401c47fe4bb90f001e1935a8eb6bd27e69f260e4847c8b72e5d"} {"question" "q-75117" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $A?$\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/state.json" "file_sha256" "0cb6e4900c271401c47fe4bb90f001e1935a8eb6bd27e69f260e4847c8b72e5d"} {"question" "q-75117" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $A,$ we write $A=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:  \n\n$$\nA=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the left is not a unit lower triangular matrix, as it has an entry not equal to $1$  on the main diagonal.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The second product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the right is not an upper triangular matrix, as it has a non-zero entry below the diagonal. \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The third product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/state.json" "file_sha256" "0cb6e4900c271401c47fe4bb90f001e1935a8eb6bd27e69f260e4847c8b72e5d"} {"question" "q-75117" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3e0e5c10da6ead45c0e8bd01f0506e038c195bfddc01dae4e7fb2063d729afef"} {"question" "q-75117" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/state.json" "file_sha256" "0cb6e4900c271401c47fe4bb90f001e1935a8eb6bd27e69f260e4847c8b72e5d"} {"question" "q-75117" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/state.json" "file_sha256" "0cb6e4900c271401c47fe4bb90f001e1935a8eb6bd27e69f260e4847c8b72e5d"} {"question" "q-75117" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/state.json" "file_sha256" "0cb6e4900c271401c47fe4bb90f001e1935a8eb6bd27e69f260e4847c8b72e5d"} {"question" "q-106751" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $A?$\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/state.json" "file_sha256" "0cb6e4900c271401c47fe4bb90f001e1935a8eb6bd27e69f260e4847c8b72e5d"} {"question" "q-106751" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $A,$ we write $A=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nA=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The second product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the left is not a unit lower triangular matrix, as its main diagonal contains a number not equal to $1.$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The third product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the right is not an upper triangular matrix, as it has a non-zero entry below the diagonal.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/state.json" "file_sha256" "0cb6e4900c271401c47fe4bb90f001e1935a8eb6bd27e69f260e4847c8b72e5d"} {"question" "q-106751" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Cou