={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-5,{u}_{22}=6,$ and ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{12}=-30.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/state.json" "file_sha256" "0cb6e4900c271401c47fe4bb90f001e1935a8eb6bd27e69f260e4847c8b72e5d"} {"question" "q-106762" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3e0e5c10da6ead45c0e8bd01f0506e038c195bfddc01dae4e7fb2063d729afef"} {"question" "q-106762" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/state.json" "file_sha256" "0cb6e4900c271401c47fe4bb90f001e1935a8eb6bd27e69f260e4847c8b72e5d"} {"question" "q-106762" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-18"] ["math" "-24"] ["math" "-25"] ["math" "-30"] ["math" "-35"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/state.json" "file_sha256" "0cb6e4900c271401c47fe4bb90f001e1935a8eb6bd27e69f260e4847c8b72e5d"} {"question" "q-106762" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-30"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/state.json" "file_sha256" "0cb6e4900c271401c47fe4bb90f001e1935a8eb6bd27e69f260e4847c8b72e5d"} {"question" "q-32617" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that \n$B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 9 \\\\ 7 & 20\\end{aligned}]$ \nhas the LU decomposition $B=LU$, then ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{12}=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/state.json" "file_sha256" "0cb6e4900c271401c47fe4bb90f001e1935a8eb6bd27e69f260e4847c8b72e5d"} {"question" "q-32617" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $B$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $B$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 9 \\\\ 7 & 20\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{7}{3}){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{3} & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot B \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}B=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}B & =U \\\\ B & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ B & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{3} & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ B & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{7}{3} & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=\\frac{7}{3},{u}_{12}=9,$ and ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{12}=21.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/state.json" "file_sha256" "0cb6e4900c271401c47fe4bb90f001e1935a8eb6bd27e69f260e4847c8b72e5d"} {"question" "q-32617" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3e0e5c10da6ead45c0e8bd01f0506e038c195bf