}+{u}_{22}=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/state.json" "file_sha256" "0cb6e4900c271401c47fe4bb90f001e1935a8eb6bd27e69f260e4847c8b72e5d"} {"question" "q-75179" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 9 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-3,{u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=-3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/state.json" "file_sha256" "0cb6e4900c271401c47fe4bb90f001e1935a8eb6bd27e69f260e4847c8b72e5d"} {"question" "q-75179" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3e0e5c10da6ead45c0e8bd01f0506e038c195bfddc01dae4e7fb2063d729afef"} {"question" "q-75179" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/state.json" "file_sha256" "0cb6e4900c271401c47fe4bb90f001e1935a8eb6bd27e69f260e4847c8b72e5d"} {"question" "q-75179" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-3"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/state.json" "file_sha256" "0cb6e4900c271401c47fe4bb90f001e1935a8eb6bd27e69f260e4847c8b72e5d"} {"question" "q-75179" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/state.json" "file_sha256" "0cb6e4900c271401c47fe4bb90f001e1935a8eb6bd27e69f260e4847c8b72e5d"} {"question" "q-106773" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/state.json" "file_sha256" "0cb6e4900c271401c47fe4bb90f001e1935a8eb6bd27e69f260e4847c8b72e5d"} {"question" "q-106773" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(\\frac{2}{3}){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-\\frac{2}{3},$ ${u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=-\\frac{2}{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/state.json" "file_sha256" "0cb6e4900c271401c47fe4bb90f001e1935a8eb6bd27e69f260e4847c8b72e5d"} {"question" "q-106773" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3e0e5c10da6ead45c0e8bd01f0506e038c195bfddc01dae4e7fb2063d729afef"} {"question" "q-106773" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/state.json" "file_sha256" "0cb6e4900c271401c47fe4bb90f001e1935a8eb6bd27e69f260e4847c8b72e5d"} {"question" "q-106773" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "-\\frac{2}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/state.json" "file_sha256" "0cb6e4900c271401c47fe4bb90f001e1935a8eb6bd27e69f260e4847c8b72e5d"} {"question" "q-106773" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{2}{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/state.json" "file_sha256" "0cb6e4900c271401c47fe4bb90f001e1935a8eb6bd27e69f260e4847c8b72e5d"} {"question" "q-106769" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 12 & -3\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/state.json" "file_sha256" "0cb6e4900c271401c47fe4bb90f001e1935a8eb6bd27e69f260e4847c8b72e5d"} {"question" "q-106769" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 12 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=3,$ ${u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/state.json" "file_sha256" "0cb6e4900c271401c47fe4bb90f001e1935a8eb6bd27e69f260e4847c8b72e5d"} {"question" "q-106769" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3e0e5c10da6ead45c0e8bd01f0506e038c195bfddc01dae4e7fb2063d729afef"} {"question" "q-106769" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/state.json" "file_sha256" "0cb6e4900c271401c47fe4bb90f001e1935a8eb6bd27e69f260e4847c8b72e5d"} {"question" "q-106769" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/state.json" "file_sha256" "0cb6e4900c271401c47fe4bb90f001e1935a8eb6bd27e69f260e4847c8b72e5d"} {"question" "q-106769" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/state.json" "file_sha256" "0cb6e4900c271401c47fe4bb90f001e1935a8eb6bd27e69f260e4847c8b72e5d"} {"question" "q-75177" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & \\,\\,\\,-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/state.json" "file_sha256" "0cb6e4900c271401c47fe4bb90f001e1935a8eb6bd27e69f260e4847c8b72e5d"} {"question" "q-75177" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & \\,\\,\\,-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-\\frac{1}{2},$ ${u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/state.json" "file_sha256" "0cb6e4900c271401c47fe4bb90f001e1935a8eb6bd27e69f260e4847c8b72e5d"} {"question" "q-75177" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3e0e5c10da6ead45c0e8bd01f0506e038c195bfddc01dae4e7fb2063d729afef"} {"question" "q-75177" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/state.json" "file_sha256" "0cb6e4900c271401c47fe4bb90f001e1935a8eb6bd27e69f260e4847c8b72e5d"} {"question" "q-75177" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/state.json" "file_sha256" "0cb6e4900c271401c47fe4bb90f001e1935a8eb6bd27e69f260e4847c8b72e5d"} {"question" "q-75177" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/state.json" "file_sha256" "0cb6e4900c271401c47fe4bb90f001e1935a8eb6bd27e69f260e4847c8b72e5d"} {"question" "q-106767" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 9 \\\\ -4 & -18\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/state.json" "file_sha256" "0cb6e4900c271401c47fe4bb90f001e1935a8eb6bd27e69f260e4847c8b72e5d"} {"question" "q-106767" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 9 \\\\ -4 & -18\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-2,$ ${u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=-2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/state.json" "file_sha256" "0cb6e4900c271401c47fe4bb90f001e1935a8eb6bd27e69f260e4847c8b72e5d"} {"question" "q-106767" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3e0e5c10da6ead45c0e8bd01f0506e038c195bfddc01dae4e7fb2063d729afef"} {"question" "q-106767" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/state.json" "file_sha256" "0cb6e4900c271401c47fe4bb90f001e1935a8eb6bd27e69f260e4847c8b72e5d"} {"question" "q-106767" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/state.json" "file_sha256" "0cb6e4900c271401c47fe4bb90f001e1935a8eb6bd27e69f260e4847c8b72e5d"} {"question" "q-106767" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/state.json" "file_sha256" "0cb6e4900c271401c47fe4bb90f001e1935a8eb6bd27e69f260e4847c8b72e5d"} {"question" "e-6184" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $A?$\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/assets/f07498a45503a02fa9fb4d3ecfd9bdae54a363b632152016f2f4e5c3f6170656.png)\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/assets/300278e3edb64ab9dcb945668b923f385b9873be5bd33264a7ed10f6072262b7.png)\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/assets/e26513d3753cd1437a42a9f57f160fb25e2fb9b84e1df1db07579429749eea2c.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/example-6184.json" "file_sha256" "8a3e90649d1853fbf4f7f4275252cdee1236f3927e7931d373aefecb90d1737d"} {"question" "e-6184" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $A,$ we write $A=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/assets/98d64291b8e0d4effe6bb1f3006c3d88d2687697355a83c1b092cdfb54ba4188.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/assets/10a251d4445a3b190e92fa179badfb5e989b577f3c88812334e921522e7a20e5.png)\n\n- The second product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although the left matrix is a lower triangular matrix, it is not a *unit* lower triangular matrix because it does not have $1$'s on the diagonal.\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/assets/515f139b62a15ee4b38066e6225066b527e277e904ea97473d2a9f50f0f595cb.png)\n\n- The third product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the right is not an upper triangular matrix, as it has a non-zero entry below the diagonal.\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/assets/e377b0ff63ac6d7b3938187e47a3b5bca0087ddb6eee1d2c086835f394cdf6b9.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/example-6184.json" "file_sha256" "8a3e90649d1853fbf4f7f4275252cdee1236f3927e7931d373aefecb90d1737d"} {"question" "e-4765" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/assets/98496fe489edc905b587f42825bd0ef76dffa68bece7e204abae6de259501835.png) find its LU decomposition." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/example-4765.json" "file_sha256" "52d47cf956015e1a282c2aa892fed2bf4ca0665163f8e3ad79413807110286e5"} {"question" "e-4765" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 9 \\\\ -7 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+1\\cdot {R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}7 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}7 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/example-4765.json" "file_sha256" "52d47cf956015e1a282c2aa892fed2bf4ca0665163f8e3ad79413807110286e5"} {"question" "e-4767" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/assets/4a8b4b988e2f4c93881f577ce12f2aa59a4dbcc00bf4122e23873255c27c2d72.png), find its LU decomposition." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/example-4767.json" "file_sha256" "97357febd5dd9c329491f2316013305c9d2118297386e15766a780b837b569a2"} {"question" "e-4767" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 12 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/example-4767.json" "file_sha256" "97357febd5dd9c329491f2316013305c9d2118297386e15766a780b837b569a2"}] "usage" {"e-6184" [] "q-32549" [] "e-4767" [] "e-4765" []} "basis" 1906 "retractions" [[17592186120118 :question/problem "Given that\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -16 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$\nhas the LU decomposition $A = LU$, then $L =$"] [17592186119592 :question/problem "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $A$?\n\n1. $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n2. $\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$\n3. $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186119592 :question/worked-solution "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $A$, we write $A=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n- The second product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although the left matrix is a lower triangular matrix, it is not a *unit* lower triangular matrix because it does not have $1$'s on the diagonal.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$\n- The third product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the right is not an upper triangular matrix, as it has a non-zero entry below the diagonal.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [17592186119530 :question/problem "Given $\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ -7 & -6\\end{bmatrix}$ find its LU decomposition."] [17592186119530 :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U$, we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ -7 & -6\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} \\mid:=R_{2} + 1 \\cdot R_{1}⟺\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(E_{1}) \\cdot A \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= U\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U$. Now, since $E_1A=U$, we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE_{1}A &= U \\\\\nA &= E_{1}^{-1}U \\\\\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}U \\\\\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(L)\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(U)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186119531 :question/problem "Given\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 12 & 9\\end{bmatrix}$, find its LU decomposition."] [17592186119531 :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U$, we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 12 & 9\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} \\mid:=R_{2} + (-3)R_{1}⟺\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(E_{1}) \\cdot A \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= U\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U$. Now, since $E_1A=U$, we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE_{1}A &= U \\\\\nA &= E_{1}^{-1}U \\\\\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}U \\\\\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(L)\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(U)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186117229 17592186117230 17592186117231 17592186117232 17592186117233] "immutable_fields" [[17592186117228 :answer-field/correct] [17592186117228 :answer-field/id] [17592186117228 :answer-field/key] [17592186117228 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
